```markdown
# Loom: Lares (Tasked Spirit)
## Off-main-thread application engines and projected UI — what the field knows

Read through all five Mu tools; where the field's answer is a `0` ("no one has settled this"), I say so rather than fill it.

---

## 1. PRIOR ART

### 1.1 AMP WorkerDOM — the closest analogue, and it is close

WorkerDOM is still alive (last push 2026-09-02). Its protocol is worth reading in full because it is *your* problem, solved by Google, seven years deep. [`src/transfer/TransferrableMutation.ts`](https://github.com/ampproject/worker-dom/blob/main/src/transfer/TransferrableMutation.ts)

**Wire shape.** Not JSON, not HTML. Each frame is `{strings: Array<string>, nodes: ArrayBuffer, mutations: ArrayBuffer}` — a **string intern table** plus two `Uint16Array`s of positional opcodes. Fifteen mutation kinds (`ATTRIBUTES`, `CHARACTER_DATA`, `CHILD_LIST`, `PROPERTIES`, `EVENT_SUBSCRIPTION`, `GET_BOUNDING_CLIENT_RECT`, `LONG_TASK_START/END`, `STORAGE`, `FUNCTION_CALL`, `SCROLL_INTO_VIEW`, …). ([`Messages.ts`](https://github.com/ampproject/worker-dom/blob/main/src/transfer/Messages.ts))

**Node identity is a monotone integer, stamped on the real node as `_index_`.** Not a path, not an HTML `id`. Hydration walks the server-rendered tree in traversal order and assigns `++count`; the worker's `document`/`body` are pinned to indices 1 and 2. The main thread keeps `Map<number, Node>` forever. ([`main-thread/nodes.ts`](https://github.com/ampproject/worker-dom/blob/main/src/main-thread/nodes.ts))

**Events: the handler never crosses; the *subscription* crosses.** `addEventListener` in the worker emits an `EVENT_SUBSCRIPTION` mutation; the main thread attaches a real listener that serializes an allowlist and posts it back. The allowlist is exactly: `bubbles, cancelable, cancelBubble, currentTarget, defaultPrevented, eventPhase, isTrusted, returnValue, target, timeStamp, type, keyCode, pageX/Y, offsetX/Y, touches, changedTouches`. Nothing else. ([`event-subscription.ts`](https://github.com/ampproject/worker-dom/blob/main/src/main-thread/commands/event-subscription.ts), [`TransferrableEvent.ts`](https://github.com/ampproject/worker-dom/blob/main/src/transfer/TransferrableEvent.ts))

**The `preventDefault` answer — this is the single most important design fact in the corpus.** Because the worker handler runs *later*, it cannot cancel anything. So WorkerDOM moved the decision to **subscription time**: `AddEventRegistrationIndex.WorkerDOMPreventDefault` is a flag in the subscribe message, and the main-thread shim does `if (preventDefault) event.preventDefault()` *before* relaying. Correspondingly, in the compatibility table `Event.preventDefault()`, `Event.stopPropagation()`, `Event.stopImmediatePropagation()`, and `EventTarget.dispatchEvent()` are all **✖️ unimplemented** — the worker-side event object is inert for control flow. ([`web_compat_table.md`](https://github.com/ampproject/worker-dom/blob/main/web_compat_table.md))

**Input value: whole value, three redundant paths, never a diff.** A separate `MessageType.SYNC` carries `{index, value}`. It fires (a) from a **default `oninput` listener the main thread installs on every node where `'value' in node`**, whether or not the worker subscribed; (b) from *any* relayed event whose `currentTarget` has a value; (c) from a **`MutationObserver` on attributes**, because frameworks set value via attribute. Your text-leg design is exactly this, minus (c).

**Coalescing.** The default `mutationPump` is **`requestAnimationFrame`** — frame pinning is WorkerDOM's default, and it is pluggable so the host can defer or reject. But the queue is **append-and-flush**, not newest-wins, because the payload is *deltas*. Their own comment names the cliff: *"can cause jank if there are too many mutations to apply in a single frame. Investigations in using asyncFlush to resolve are worth considering."* ([`mutator.ts`](https://github.com/ampproject/worker-dom/blob/main/src/main-thread/mutator.ts))

**What they never solved.** `HTMLElement.focus()` ✖️. `blur()` ✖️. `activeElement` ✖️. `getSelection()` ✖️. `createRange()` ✖️. `onselectionchange` ✖️. `Document.head` ✖️. `hasFocus()` ✖️. `CustomEvent.detail` ✖️. `dataset` ✖️. All geometry sync-reads ✖️ — `getBoundingClientRect` was **renamed to `getBoundingClientRectAsync()` returning a Promise** that defaults to all-zeros. Seven years, and programmatic focus and caret from the worker are simply absent.

**AMP's own guardrails, layered on top:** mutations only within 5 s of a user gesture (unless fixed-size container); `<script>`/`<style>` injection removed silently; forms need `sandbox="allow-forms"`; "terminated due to illegal mutation" on repeat offence; 150 KB script budget. ([amp.dev](https://amp.dev/documentation/components/amp-script))

### 1.2 Shopify remote-dom — and the rewrite lesson

Four mutation kinds and nothing else: `INSERT_CHILD, REMOVE_CHILD, UPDATE_TEXT, UPDATE_PROPERTY`, as positional tuples; root id is the literal `'~'`. Properties, attributes, and event listeners are one mutation kind discriminated by a trailing type tag. ([constants.ts](https://github.com/Shopify/remote-dom/blob/main/packages/core/source/constants.ts))

**Why they rewrote remote-ui.** remote-ui invented its own tree; every framework then needed a bespoke reconciler, and Vue support suffered. remote-dom made remote components **plain custom elements** and used the browser's own `MutationObserver` as the change detector — *"any library built to work with the DOM now works without any special adaptors."* ([discussion #267](https://github.com/Shopify/remote-dom/discussions/267))

**Functions across the boundary cost memory, permanently.** `@quilted/threads`, verbatim: *"Implementing functions using message passing always leaks memory."* Mitigation is `WeakRef`+`FinalizationRegistry` or manual `retain()`/`release()`, which remote-dom surfaces as constructor options. ([threads README](https://github.com/lemonmade/quilt/blob/main/packages/threads/README.md))

**Listener registration is itself a wire message.** Remote elements declare `static get remoteEvents()`, and *"the remote element will not even request that the host inform it of a particular non-bubbling event, unless an event listener for that event is specifically added."* You pay only for what someone subscribed to.

### 1.3 Phoenix LiveView — the strongest engineering in the survey

Correction to the brief: **LiveView still uses morphdom**; it pushed changes *upstream into* morphdom rather than replacing it. ([1.1 release post](https://www.phoenixframework.org/blog/phoenix-liveview-1-1-released))

**The out-of-order answer: clocks + locks + a clone tree.** A monotone counter identifies each in-flight event and ties it to stateful elements via `PHX_REF_LOCK`. Patches for a locked element are morphed into a **private cloned tree**; on ack the clone is morphed back. *"The JavaScript client is always the source of truth for current input values."* ([syncing-changes guide](https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix_live_view/syncing-changes.html), [`dom_patch.ts`](https://github.com/phoenixframework/phoenix_live_view/blob/main/assets/js/phoenix_live_view/dom_patch.ts)) This machinery generated its own bug class: [#3313](https://github.com/phoenixframework/phoenix_live_view/pull/3313), [#3942](https://github.com/phoenixframework/phoenix_live_view/pull/3942), [#4134](https://github.com/phoenixframework/phoenix_live_view/pull/4134).

**Focus: excluded from patching by default.** *"Focused form controls are not patched by default, so that client-side input always wins over potentially stale server updates."* The opt-*in* (`phx-patch-focused`) arrived only in v1.2.8. Selection range is captured before the morph and restored after.

**IME: the cure was on the outbound leg.** [#3322](https://github.com/phoenixframework/phoenix_live_view/issues/3322) — Japanese Romaji in a Safari textarea produced `dえ` instead of `で` when a patch landed mid-composition; **Safari-only, and only mid-text**. Fix ([#3332](https://github.com/phoenixframework/phoenix_live_view/pull/3332)): *ignore input events fired during composition and emit once after `compositionend`.*

**Form recovery**: forms with `phx-change` + an `id` replay the change event after remount. Missing `id` silently disables it — enough that they added a compile-time warning. [#4086](https://github.com/phoenixframework/phoenix_live_view/issues/4086): recovery silently fails when every input has its own `phx-change`.

**They ship a latency simulator** (`liveSocket.enableLatencySim(1000)`) because *"near zero latency on localhost does not allow latency to be easily represented or tested."*
```

