### 1.4 Hotwire/Turbo + the morphdom→idiomorph lineage

37signals' Jorge Manrubia, on why morphing won — the most transferable sentence in the corpus:

> *"I initially thought that it was improving rendering speed, but, in reality, the improvement came from **keeping client-side state**: scroll, focus, selected text, CSS transition states, etc."* ([dev.37signals.com](https://dev.37signals.com/a-happier-happy-path-in-turbo-with-morphing))

They dropped morphdom because it required *"adding ids everywhere to help the algorithm match nodes."* idiomorph's fix: **id-sets** — map each element to the set of all ids in its subtree; two elements match if their id-sets intersect. An id anywhere anchors its ancestors. Cost ~10% on large morphs. ([idiomorph README](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph))

Documented morph breakages (all the same disease — **live state with no attribute representation**):
- input `value` cleared by a morph that carries no `value` attribute ([#27](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/issues/27), [#132](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/issues/132)) → `ignoreActiveValue`
- **web components never get `connectedCallback`/`disconnectedCallback`** ([#57](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/issues/57); same gap unanswered in morphdom [#127](https://github.com/patrick-steele-idem/morphdom/issues/127))
- `<dialog>` opened with `showModal()` — the **top layer** is not in the DOM tree, so the page becomes non-interactive under an empty overlay, with *"no api to interact directly with the top layer"* ([turbo#1239](https://github.com/hotwired/turbo/issues/1239))
- **DOM clobbering**: `.id` on a `<form>` containing `<input name="id">` returns the *child*, silently breaking matching ([#136](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/pull/136))
- scroll in overflow containers ([#26](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/issues/26), open); SVG needing `createElementNS` ([#154](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/pull/154))
- **Shadow DOM and iframes: explicitly listed as unexamined.** ([PR #93](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/pull/93)) No source found for a resolution.

**Move, don't delete-and-re-add.** [idiomorph #61](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/pull/61): reordering 1,2,3,4,5 → 5,1,2,3,4 deleted items 1–4 and destroyed an in-progress `textarea`. Fix: `Element.before` / native `moveBefore`.

**Focus restore is a fallback that emits its own events.** PR #93, verbatim: *"maintaining focus and selection state in all cases is impossible… we can paper over this by saving and restoring focus and selection states around the morph, **at the cost of potential extra blur, focus, and selection events**."* It then generated [#127](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/pull/127), [#151](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/issues/151), [#138](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/idiomorph/issues/138).

Turbo needed **two** veto hooks, not one: `turbo:before-morph-element` *and* `turbo:before-morph-attribute` ([PR #1097](https://github.com/hotwired/turbo/pull/1097)) — and there are open requests for more.

### 1.5 Blazor Server — the opposite answer, and the warnings

Microsoft's published limits: per-tab circuit, per-circuit server memory, and — verbatim — *"In regular web apps, requests and connections time out as a safeguard, but Blazor apps don't provide the same safeguards."* Plus the direct one: **"Some DOM events, such as `oninput` or `onscroll`, can produce a large amount of data. Avoid using these events in server-side Blazor."** ([interactive-server-side-rendering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/security/interactive-server-side-rendering))

Where LiveView gives a *framework* guarantee (ignore clicks awaiting ack), Blazor gives a *documentation* guarantee: *"multiple events can be dispatched to the server before the app has time to react… Limiting client actions must be performed inside event handlers and not depend on the current rendered view state."*

Two mechanisms worth stealing:
- **`EventFieldInfo`** — the event payload patches the server's own render tree for the mutated field before diffing, matched by event-handler id. ([source](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/src/Components/Components/src/RenderTree/EventFieldInfo.cs))
- **Attribute order hazard**, verbatim: *"We defer setting the 'value' property for `<input>` because certain types of inputs have default attribute values that may incorrectly constrain the specified 'value'. For example, range inputs have default 'min' and 'max' attributes that may incorrectly clamp the 'value'."* ([`DomSpecialPropertyUtil.ts`](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/src/Components/Web.JS/src/Rendering/DomSpecialPropertyUtil.ts))

Blazor's focus handling is a single blind `activeElementBefore.focus()` with **no selection preservation**. Backpressure is real: it *stops producing render batches* if the client stops acking (`MaxBufferedUnacknowledgedRenderBatches`).

### 1.6 Products that own their surface — the uniform tax

- **Figma**: WebGL from scratch because *"text layout is inconsistent between browsers"* and blend/mask support *"varies wildly."* Their own listed biggest pain point: **no access to glyph outlines or kerning tables.** Wasm's 32-bit cap and grow-only memory forced over-allocation → **OOM crashes on mobile** for legitimate documents. ([Figma blog](https://www.figma.com/blog/building-a-professional-design-tool-on-the-web/), [Andrew Chan](https://andrewkchan.dev/posts/figma2.html))
- **Google Docs canvas**: shipped *"accessibility… will not be impacted"* as an assertion; **WebAIM tested it and did not accept it** ([webaim.org](https://webaim.org/blog/seismic-change-to-docs/)). Extensions lost text access entirely; the ecosystem's only way back in became **"Annotated Canvas" — undocumented, allowlisted, form-gated** ([chromium-extensions](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/chromium-extensions/c/dLnxATEOcqg)).
- **Flutter Web CanvasKit**: accessibility is a **shadow-DOM overlay, opt-in behind a hidden 1px button, off by default for performance**, absolutely positioned and re-positioned on every scroll ([flutter.dev](https://flutter.dev/blog/accessibility-in-flutter-on-the-web)); it announces *"Enable accessibility"* to screen readers even when already on ([#98160](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/issues/98160)). A 10k-line TextField: HTML renderer 0.49 s, **CanvasKit 90.3 s** ([#128575](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/issues/128575)). Ten-plus open IME issues; the purest is [#134268](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/issues/134268) — *the app cannot detect that a native IME panel is open.*
- **Qt for WASM** arrived at the *same* shadow-element overlay independently, also off by default. And documents pure silent no-ops: `setShareContext()` *"has no effect."* ([doc.qt.io](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/wasm.html))
- **Unity**: forced to instantiate a real DOM input to raise a soft keyboard, and therefore cannot hide it — `HideMobileInput` *"has no effect on the Web platform."* ([Unity manual](https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/webgl-input.html))
- **Photoshop Web split the surface**: canvas for the image, **real DOM (Lit + shadow DOM) for the chrome**. ([web.dev](https://web.dev/articles/ps-on-the-web))

### 1.7 Partytown, RSC, Million

- **Partytown** proves the other family: a *synchronous* DOM shim via `Atomics.wait` + SAB, requiring `COOP: same-origin` + `COEP: credentialless`, falling back to sync-XHR through a service worker. Its own trade-offs page: **"Events handled by third-party scripts that call `event.preventDefault()` will have no effect."** And the FAQ: *"there are also scenarios where your script may hit an internal error and **silently fail**."* Andrea Giammarchi's `coincident` measures the fallback cliff: **~1,000 round-trips/sec polyfilled vs 50,000+ native**, plus deadlock risk. ([trade-offs](https://partytown.qwik.dev/trade-offs/), [atomics](https://partytown.qwik.dev/atomics/), [coincident](https://github.com/WebReflection/coincident)) Status: `@builder.io/partytown` is deprecated in favour of `@qwik.dev/partytown`, which shipped 0.14.3 on 2026-08-25 — the project moved, it did not die.
- **RSC** landed the same law from the other end: functions are not serializable; the only callable that crosses is a **reference id** redeemed by an async round trip (`{$$typeof: Symbol.for("react.server.reference"), $$id: ...}`). ([react.dev](https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-client))
- **Million.js** is de facto retired — npm 3.1.11 (2024-06), million.dev no longer mentions it.
- **Surma's structural objection**, which you should read as the field's honest `0`: *"In most scenarios, however, that just **moves** the problem. You are at risk of effectively creating a 2nd main thread, with all the same problems, just in a different thread."* ([Smashing, 2021](https://www.smashingmagazine.com/2021/06/web-workers-2021/))

### 1.8 TiddlyWiki specifically

**Nobody has done this.** Exhaustive search of talk.tiddlywiki.org, the imported Google Groups archive, and GitHub issues/PRs found **no prior art** for running the TW5 engine (boot + store + widget tree + refresh cycle) in a Worker or ServiceWorker. There is no lineage to inherit and none to collide with.

What the field *does* record:

- `$tw.fakeDocument` is **372 lines** ([fakedom.js](https://github.com/TiddlyWiki/TiddlyWiki5/blob/master/core/modules/utils/fakedom.js)). `addEventListener`'s entire body is `// Do nothing`. No `removeEventListener`, no `dispatchEvent`, no event objects. No geometry, no `querySelector`, no `classList`, no `cloneNode`, no `nextSibling`, no `ownerDocument`, no `focus`/`activeElement`. `innerHTML`'s **setter does not parse** — it sets `isRaw = true` and stores the string.
- It is **already used in the browser**, not only server-side: `wiki.makeWidget` defaults to it, and the page `<title>` + the whole `$:/core/ui/PageStylesheet` pipeline render into it (`core/modules/startup/render.js`). The dev docs say so: *"Because the fake DOM is faster than the real DOM, it is also used in the browser when interactive rendering to the DOM isn't needed."*
- **Exactly five core sites check `isTiddlyWikiFakeDom`**: `editor/factory.js:211` (forces the SimpleEngine, never the iframe FramedEngine), `simple.js:72,105`, `framed.js:126,161`, `list.js:58` (storyviews skipped), `reveal.js:45,279` (popup positioning + animation skipped).
- The framed editor exists for one reason, from its own header: *"This is done so that the selection is preserved even when clicking away from the textarea."*
- Focus is message-driven: `tm-focus-selector` → `document.querySelector(sel).focus()` at `startup/rootwidget.js:80-88`. There is **no `$tw.utils.focusSelector`** — no source found for that name.
- Three hard `ReferenceError`s in a Worker, none architectural: `bootprefix.js:30/33` (TW5 has only two platforms; a Worker is neither), `utils.js:706-713` `$tw.utils.nextTick` → `window.setTimeout` (**hit on the first store write**, via `wiki.js:161`), `utils.js:869-876` `$tw.utils.timer` → `window.performance`. Plus `boot.js:166-168` `domMaker`'s implicit global `document`.
- **Refresh-cycle economics, measured by the community**: 100–300 ms per keypress with a few large tiddlers open, and *"After 40 open tiddlers, it takes about 500 ms to open the next one"* ([talk t/4185](https://talk.tiddlywiki.org/t/4185)). 8,900-tiddler wiki: `mainRefresh` 100–200 ms typical, 400 ms for nested tabs ([t/4040](https://talk.tiddlywiki.org/t/4040)). Only **drafts** are debounced (400 ms `THROTTLE_REFRESH_TIMEOUT`); every other change refreshes synchronously.
- Open [PR #9728](https://github.com/TiddlyWiki/TiddlyWiki5/pull/9728) is a **headless refresh-cycle recorder/replayer** (`--perf-replay`) — a ready-made instrument for exactly this architecture.
- Precedent that the fakedom seam is swappable: [talk t/606](https://talk.tiddlywiki.org/t/606), where linkedom was substituted successfully and **Jeremy Ruston said he had considered a jsdom-swap plugin himself**.
