---

## 2. GOLDEN PRINCIPLES

**Node identity.** Every survivor uses an **opaque, engine-allocated, monotone handle stamped onto the node** (WorkerDOM `_index_`; remote-dom string ids; Blazor event-handler ids) — never a DOM path, never a semantic `id`. Paths break under sibling shifts; semantic ids collide with author markup and with **DOM clobbering** (`<form>` + `<input name="id">` → `.id` returns the child). Where matching *is* structural, id-sets beat bare ids because an id anywhere anchors its ancestors. And an id that is *re-set to its own value* can itself invalidate matching ([LiveView #4325](https://github.com/phoenixframework/phoenix_live_view/issues/4325)).

**Event serialization.** Ship an allowlist, not the event. WorkerDOM's is 17 fields. Two hard consequences of the handler running later and elsewhere:
- **`preventDefault` must be declared at subscription time, not decided at handler time.** Partytown simply has none. WorkerDOM registers a boolean per listener. LiveView always preventDefaults locally and compensates with loading state.
- **`beforeinput` with `inputType: "insertCompositionText"` is spec-defined as NOT cancelable** ([Input Events L2](https://w3c.github.io/input-events/)) — the IME is out of your reach by design.
- **User activation does not travel.** Transient activation is a per-`Window` timestamp, consumed by activation-gated APIs, and *"expected to be at most a few seconds"* ([HTML spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#transient-activation)). Clipboard, fullscreen, `window.open`, Web Share, PiP, payment all require it. A worker has none, and by the time a relayed event round-trips it may be gone. AMP's 5-second post-gesture mutation window is the same idea wearing different clothes.
- **Ordering is already guaranteed** on a single port — the HTML spec makes the port message queue a FIFO task source ([web-messaging](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-messaging.html)). Reordering only appears across *multiple* channels or async interleaving.

**Coalescing / backpressure.** Newest-wins is sound **only when the payload is absolute state**. Every delta-based system (WorkerDOM, remote-dom, Blazor, LiveView) must queue, because dropping a delta corrupts. rAF is WorkerDOM's default pump, but it is *pluggable* so the host can defer or refuse. `postMessage` has **no backpressure at all** — that is why Streams exist ([MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Streams_API/Concepts)); a producer faster than the consumer grows the queue without bound. Blazor's answer is the explicit ack window (`MaxBufferedUnacknowledgedRenderBatches`). Note also that a fixed timer window is **throttled to 1 s (then 1 min) in a hidden tab** ([Chrome 88](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/timer-throttling-in-chrome-88)).

**Input / caret / focus.**
- **`value` is a property, not an attribute.** Once the user types, the input's **dirty value flag** is set and *"if the control's dirty value flag is false"* gates attribute→value propagation ([HTML spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html)). A serialized `value="…"` in a projected frame is **ignored** thereafter. Same class: `checked`, `<select>` selection, `<video>` playback, scroll offsets, `<dialog>` top layer, CSS transition state.
- **Exclude the focused element from patching by default** — LiveView's default; the opt-in came later. Save/restore is the fallback, and it *"emits extra blur, focus, and selection events."*
- `setSelectionRange` **throws `InvalidStateError`** on `email`/`number`/`date` inputs ([MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLInputElement/setSelectionRange)) — restore code that works on `text` crashes on a numeric field.
- **IME**: guard the *outbound* leg. LiveView's fix was to suppress `input` relay while `isComposing` and emit once at `compositionend`. Vue documents the same seam from the other side: *"v-model doesn't get updated during IME composition"* ([vuejs.org](https://vuejs.org/guide/essentials/forms.html)). Expect Safari-specific behaviour and mid-text-only reproduction.

**Idempotence.** Whole-value beats diff, universally. WorkerDOM's `TransferrableSyncValue` is `{index, value}` — no history, no diff, and it is fired from three redundant triggers because a missed value silently desynchronizes the engine's model.

---

## 3. ANTI-PATTERNS AND KNOWN FAILURE MODES

**The engine assumes things about its environment that a fake document cannot supply.** TW5's `reveal.js` walks `offsetParent`/`offsetWidth`; `button.js` calls `getBoundingClientRect` **without a guard** (`action-navigate.js` guards, `button.js` does not); `scroller.js` needs `querySelector`. Fifteen geometry sites in TW5 core. WorkerDOM's answer after seven years is an async promise defaulting to zeros.

**Silent degradation is the characteristic failure, not error.** Qt: `setShareContext()` *"has no effect."* Unity: `HideMobileInput` *"has no effect on the Web platform."* Partytown: *"may hit an internal error and silently fail."* A dev server without COOP/COEP silently yields the single-threaded build. TW5: `setAttributeNS` in fakedom **discards the namespace entirely** (body is literally `this.setAttribute(name,value)`), so `xlink:href` serializes as `href`; `removeChild` splices the array but **never clears `parentNode`**, so a detached node still tests as attached. LiveView #4169: an id-less sibling shift silently detaches a hook's cached node and infinite scroll just stops.

**Works under test doubles, breaks against real input.** jsdom, verbatim, does not implement *"the ability to calculate where elements will be visually laid out… which impacts methods like `getBoundingClientRects()` or properties like `offsetTop`"* — it **returns zeros** ([jsdom](https://github.com/jsdom/jsdom)). So does WorkerDOM's async rect. Synthetic events carry `isTrusted: false` and `HTMLElement.click()` does too ([MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Event/isTrusted)) — activation-gated APIs will refuse them. TW5's entire widget test suite runs on fakedom, which by construction cannot exercise focus, selection, geometry, or events.

**Accessibility.** Two independent projects (Flutter, Qt) converged on the same workaround — an **opt-in, off-by-default, absolutely-positioned shadow-DOM overlay covering a subset of roles** — and Flutter's races with the screen reader. Then shadow DOM adds its own tax: **ARIA IDREFs do not cross shadow boundaries** — `aria-labelledby`, `aria-describedby`, `aria-controls`, `aria-activedescendant`, `aria-owns`, `label for`. Combobox and listbox patterns become inoperable. *"Accessibility with shadow roots is broken."* The standards fix (`ariaLabelledByElements`) takes **element references, which cannot be expressed in serialized HTML at all**, and *"doesn't work with declarative shadow DOM"* ([Nolan Lawson](https://nolanlawson.com/2022/11/28/shadow-dom-and-accessibility-the-trouble-with-aria/)). Separately: **a live region must exist in the DOM *before* its content changes** — *"Start with an empty live region, then – in a separate step – change the content"* ([MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Accessibility/ARIA/Guides/Live_regions)); a frame that re-creates the region node each time announces nothing.

**Selection and shadow DOM is a ten-year-old open question.** [WICG/webcomponents#79](https://github.com/WICG/webcomponents/issues/79), opened 2015, still open: *"`window.getSelection()` may never access shadow DOM content"*; selection spanning a boundary is unspecified. `document.activeElement` returns the **shadow host**, not the focused element ([MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/activeElement)).

**Security of injected HTML.** `innerHTML` *"is probably the most common vector for XSS"*; it blocks `<script>` but not `<img src=x onerror=…>` ([MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/innerHTML)). **A shadow root is not a security boundary** — I could find no spec or MDN text claiming it is, and the DOM spec's closed-mode language is carefully behavioural, not security-framed. AMP does not trust its own worker: it runs every mutation through a `Sanitizer` and removes `<script>`/`<style>` unconditionally. Note also that `setHTMLUnsafe` parses declarative shadow roots where `innerHTML` does not — a serialized frame containing `<template shadowrootmode>` behaves differently depending on which sink you use.

**Performance cliffs.** WorkerDOM's own comment on sync flush jank. Lighthouse flags **>800 nodes** as a warning, **>1,400** as an error ([Chrome](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/lighthouse/performance/dom-size/)). CanvasKit's 90-second text field. TW5's 500 ms-per-tiddler at 40 open tiddlers. `postMessage` itself is *not* the bottleneck below ~100 KiB — Surma's measurements give **≤100 KiB inside a 100 ms budget, ≤10 KiB inside a 16 ms frame** ([surma.dev](https://surma.dev/things/is-postmessage-slow/)) — but **serialization blocks the sender and deserialization blocks the receiver**, and Chrome/Safari defer deserialization until `event.data` is read while Firefox does it before dispatch, so *when* you pay is browser-dependent. Node maps that grow monotonically (WorkerDOM's) and function-proxy tables (`@quilted/threads`: *"always leaks memory"*) are the two standard leaks.

**Debuggability.** MDN documents the Worker `error` event as *"a generic `Event`"* — no structured error, no stack crosses the boundary. Partytown's only debugging affordance is a set of log flags.

---

## 4. WHAT TO MEASURE

Instruments others found necessary, in rough order of what they caught:

1. **INP, decomposed into its three phases** — input delay / processing / presentation ([web.dev](https://web.dev/articles/inp); good ≤200 ms, poor >500 ms at p75). In this architecture the presentation phase contains a full worker round trip, so the decomposition is the diagnosis.
2. **`PerformanceEventTiming`** with `durationThreshold: 16` — gives `processingStart - startTime` (delay) and `processingEnd - processingStart` (handler) per interaction. Caveat that bites here: **`entry.target` is null if the element was removed**, so morphing destroys your own attribution.
3. **Long Animation Frames** — `blockingDuration`, `renderStart`, `styleAndLayoutStart`, and the per-script `scripts` array. This is the only instrument that sees frame-level cumulative work rather than single long tasks ([Chrome](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/web-platform/long-animation-frames)).
4. **WorkerDOM's `LONG_TASK_START` / `LONG_TASK_END` mutation types** — the engine tells the host when it is busy, in-band. Worth copying: it is instrumentation as a first-class protocol message.
5. **A user-visible vs non-user-visible mutation split** — WorkerDOM's `isUserVisibleMutation()`. It lets you count and gate the traffic that can actually move pixels separately from bookkeeping.
6. **A latency simulator.** LiveView ships one and says localhost hides the entire bug class.
7. **Queue depth and unacked-frame count** — Blazor's `MaxBufferedUnacknowledgedRenderBatches` exists because unbounded is the default failure.
8. **Frame payload size in bytes**, against Surma's 10 KiB/16 ms and 100 KiB/100 ms budgets, measured on a slow device, not a dev machine.
9. **Node-map and handler-map cardinality over time** — the two known leaks.
10. **A headless refresh replayer.** TW5 [PR #9728](https://github.com/TiddlyWiki/TiddlyWiki5/pull/9728) already builds a recorder + `--perf-replay` that measures the refresh cycle with no browser at all, and used it to find a single filter accounting for **47% of total refresh time**.
11. **DOM node count and depth** against Lighthouse's 800/1,400 thresholds.
12. **An IME conformance pass** — Japanese Romaji mid-text in Safari specifically; that is the exact configuration that reproduced LiveView #3322 and nothing else did.

---

## 5. THE FIVE THINGS MOST WORTH YOUR ATTENTION

**① `preventDefault` cannot be a decision the worker makes — it must be a subscription-time declaration.**
WorkerDOM built an entire flag into its subscribe opcode for this and marked `Event.preventDefault()`, `stopPropagation()`, `stopImmediatePropagation()`, and `dispatchEvent()` as permanently unimplemented. Partytown gave up: *"will have no effect."* Your architecture invokes the stored handler *after* the relay, which places you exactly here. Every system in the corpus that crossed a boundary either declared cancellation in advance or lost it. There is no third answer in the field.

**② The focused element and the composing element must be excluded from projection — and IME is fixed on the outbound leg, not the inbound one.**
LiveView made "don't patch focused controls" the *default* and only later added an opt-in to patch them; idiomorph shipped focus/selection restore as an admitted paper-over that *"emits extra blur, focus, and selection events"*; Blazor's blind `.focus()` with no selection restore is the version to avoid. And the IME cure that actually worked was suppressing outbound `input` between `compositionstart` and `compositionend` — because `beforeinput`/`insertCompositionText` is spec-defined as non-cancelable, the inbound side cannot be defended. Safari, mid-text, Japanese Romaji is the reproduction.

**③ Serialized HTML structurally cannot carry the state that morphing exists to preserve.**
The **dirty value flag** means a `value="…"` attribute is ignored once the user types. `checked`, `<select>` selection, `<video>` position, scroll offset, `<dialog>`'s top layer, CSS transition state, and custom-element instances have the same property. This is not a bug list — it is one law, and every morphing project in the survey rediscovered it independently and answered it the same way: **per-element *and* per-attribute veto hooks** (Turbo needed both; LiveView keeps `data-*` live even through `phx-update="ignore"`), plus **move-don't-delete** (idiomorph #61), plus **id-sets rather than bare ids**. Note also Blazor's order hazard: applying `value` before `min`/`max` silently clamps it.

**④ Shadow DOM buys you isolation and charges you accessibility, selection, and — for anything projected — the whole ARIA reference layer.**
ARIA IDREFs do not cross the boundary; the standards-track fix takes *element references*, which serialized HTML cannot express, and does not work with declarative shadow DOM. Selection across a shadow boundary has been an open question since 2015. `document.activeElement` returns the host. Live regions must pre-exist their content. Flutter and Qt both landed on an opt-in, off-by-default overlay and neither is good. Meanwhile a shadow root is *not* a security boundary — AMP sanitizes every mutation from its own worker before applying it, and `innerHTML` still executes `<img onerror>`. Photoshop Web's split is the honest counter-model: engine-owned surface for the content, **real DOM for the chrome**.

**⑤ Newest-wins is sound because your frames are absolute — protect that property, and know that the field's ordering worry is really an in-flight-echo worry.**
Every delta-based system in the corpus must queue; you can drop precisely because a frame is whole state. That is a genuine structural advantage, and the corresponding fragility is that any *delta* you ever add to the frame silently invalidates it. Two adjacent facts: single-port `postMessage` is already FIFO by spec, so out-of-order arrival is not the real risk — the real risk is LiveView's actual bug, a stale server frame overwriting a local edit the user made *after* the frame was generated, which they solved with monotone clocks + subtree locks + a clone tree, and Blazor deliberately did not solve at all (and documents the resulting security caveat). Also: `postMessage` has no backpressure, a fixed timer window is throttled to 1 s in a hidden tab, and worker `requestAnimationFrame` exists but has limited availability.

**And one negative result worth its own line:** on running TW5 itself off the main thread, there is **no prior art** — not on talk.tiddlywiki.org, not in the Google Groups archive, not in GitHub issues. The seam is unusually clean (renderer is document-parameterized via `this.document`; `makeWidget` already defaults to fakedom; fakedom is 372 swappable lines with Jeremy Ruston on record considering a jsdom-swap plugin; `$tw.boot.suppressBoot` plus split `initStartup`/`loadStartup`/`execStartup` gives embed seams). The obstacles found are three `window` vs `self` one-liners plus `domMaker`'s implicit global — with `$tw.utils.nextTick` at `utils.js:706-713` the load-bearing one, since it is reached on the **first store write**. But there is no community experience to inherit, and TW5's published refresh economics (100–300 ms per keypress, 500 ms per tiddler at 40 open) are the baseline any measurement has to beat.
